-- title:
--   Diamond operators cut out the Γ₁(M₀)∩Γ₀(t) q-expansion field
-- statement:
--   Let $M_0$ and $t$ be coprime nonzero natural numbers and set $M = M_0 t$. Write $F_1 =$ [`ModularCurve.x1FunctionField M`](def/ModularCurve_X1.html#L137) for the intermediate field of $\mathbb{Q}((q))$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ generated by `intFormRatiosC ℚ (Gamma1 M)`; it contains the quotients `intSeriesC ℚ pf / intSeriesC ℚ pg` attached to modular forms $f,g$ of a common weight $k$ on $\Gamma_1(M)$ whose $q$-expansions at $1$ are the images of integral power series $p_f,p_g$, with `intSeriesC ℚ pg ≠ 0`. For $d$ coprime to $M$, `IsDiamondAut M d σ` says of a $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $F_1$ that for all such $f,g,p_f,p_g$ and all $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M)$ with $\gamma_{00} \equiv d \pmod M$, the complex Laurent series obtained from $\sigma(p_f/p_g)$ times the $q$-expansion of $g\mid_k\gamma$ equals that of $f\mid_k\gamma$; `diamondAut M d` is a choice of such $\sigma$ when one exists and the identity otherwise. Assume such a $\sigma$ exists for every $d$ coprime to $M$. Then there is a monoid homomorphism $\delta$ from $\Delta = \ker\bigl((\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/M_0)^\times\bigr)$ to the group of $\mathbb{Q}$-algebra automorphisms of $F_1$ with $\delta(u) =$ `diamondAut M` applied to the canonical representative in $\{0,\dots,M-1\}$ of $u$, and such that an element $x$ of $F_1$ lies, as a Laurent series, in `x1x0FunctionFieldC ℚ M₀ t` — the field generated over $\mathbb{Q}$ by `intFormRatiosC ℚ (Gamma1 M₀ ⊓ Gamma0 t)` — if and only if $\delta(u)\,x = x$ for all $u \in \Delta$.
--
--   This is the statement, over $\mathbb{Q}$ and in the $q$-expansion model with rational cusp $\infty$, that the function field of $X_1(M_0t)$ is a $\Delta$-covering of that of $X(\Gamma_1(M_0) \cap \Gamma_0(t))$ for coprime $M_0,t$, the diamond operators $\langle u \rangle$, $u \in \Delta$, acting with fixed field exactly the smaller field. It is used in the study of the models `XOneP`, where the diamond action is compared with pullback–pushforward along the covering map and with the valuation-theoretic description of points.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_exists_monoidHom_diamondAut_mem_x1x0FunctionFieldC_iff.lean

